***********************
**	DConnect Daemon
**	@PACKAGE_NAME@ @PACKAGE_VERSION@
***********************


What is it?
-----------

DConnect Daemon is a D*rect Connect Hub clone for Linux. It allow people to
share their resources, i.e.: programs, graphics, all files ...


Features
--------

* fast, pure C implementation
* low memory per user consumption
* uses threads (no forking)
* nick pattern matching
* simple administration throught telnet console
* built-in web endpoint: web console, web chat and /healthcheck
* optional TLS: encrypted client port (default 412, for TLS-capable
  DC++-style clients using ssl://) and HTTPS for the web endpoint
* salted SHA-512 password hashes in dcd.users
* hub statistics (traffic, per-command counts, user rotation)
* ready-to-run Docker image (Dockerfile + docker/)
* should work on every Unix system


License
-------

GPL version 2. See COPYRIGHT file shipped with sources for details.


Requirements
------------

To compile DConnect Daemon, you will need:

* libpthread
* OpenSSL (libcrypto + libssl; SHA-512 for password hashes, the WebSocket
  handshake and optional TLS)
* CMake (min. 3.16)
* a C compiler (C17)

Instalation
-----------

In most cases this procedure should work:

$ cd @PACKAGE@-@VERSION@
$ cmake -B build -S .
$ cmake --build build
$ sudo cmake --install build

You may propably use some standard CMake options.
There are @PACKAGE@-specific options, but you propably don't have to worry 
about it.

   -DDCD_CONFIG_DIR=PATH	- where to look for configuration files
   				(default: <prefix>/etc/dcd).

   -DDEFAULT_USER=USER		- Set default user. We STRONGLY advise to make
   				your own user and put it here. Default is daemon,
				because it's present in every Unix system.

   -DDEFAULT_GROUP=GROUP	- Set default group. You should make your own group.
    				Default is set to daemon, because this group is
				present on every Unix system.

   -DDCD_BUILD_TESTS=ON/OFF	- build the unit/perf test suite (default: ON).
    				Run it with: ctest --test-dir build

   -DDCD_ENABLE_LTO=ON		- enable link-time optimization (default: OFF).
   -DDCD_ENABLE_TSAN=ON		- instrument the dcd binary with ThreadSanitizer.
   -DDCD_ENABLE_ASAN=ON		- instrument the dcd binary with AddressSanitizer.

Docker
------

A multi-stage Dockerfile builds and runs dcd (unit tests run as part of the
image build):

$ docker build -t dcd:2.0.0 .
$ docker run -d -p 411:411 -p 411:411/udp -p 511:511 -p 8080:8080 \
      -e DCD_HUBNAME="My Hub" -v dcd-conf:/etc/dcd dcd:2.0.0

The entrypoint seeds a fresh /etc/dcd volume from the pristine config, applies
DCD_* environment overrides (DCD_HUBNAME, DCD_PORT, DCD_CONS_PORT,
DCD_HTTP_PORT, DCD_USER_LIMIT, DCD_MIN_SHARE, DCD_MIN_SLOTS,
DCD_REGISTERED_ONLY, ...) and runs dcd in the foreground with a syslog forwarder
so hub logs reach the container log. dcd.adduser is available via
`docker exec -it <container> dcd.adduser`. The image HEALTHCHECK probes
/healthcheck on the web endpoint.

Usage
-----

In directory @CONFIGDIR@ you have config files. Most of them have sufficient
comments :)

console.allow	- from which hosts you can login to telnet console. By default
		here is only 127.0.0.1, but you may add other hosts too.
		Remember that this is clean tekst login!! Do this only in 
		trusted networks.

dcd.users	- file with users accounts. 
		Syntax:
			login:password:e-mail:description:permissions

		login - you know what is that :)

		password - either a clean text password or a salted SHA-512
			hash in an /etc/shadow-like format:
			$sha512$<salt>$<hex(sha512(salt+password))>
			dcd.adduser generates such hashes with a random salt
			automatically; plaintext values are still accepted.

		e-mail - this e-mail will be shown on userlist

		description - this will be shown on userlist too

		permisions - what this user can do on hub:
			c 		- access to console
			a 		- administrator
			s 		- more than administrator
			m 		- not testing minshare
			r 		- not testing minslots
			p 		- allows user to be in passive mode when passive is not allowed
			f		- allows user to specify his ip in client
			n/none  - regular user
			
dcd.banned	- users that are banned. 
		Syntax:
			include:name
				this will include a file. cannot be used in included file;	
			 
		
			IP:reason
				When user tries to enter he is disconnected after receiving reason.

dcd.conf	- main configuration of daemon. Look in config comments for details.

dcd.motd	- message that user will see after logging in.

dcd.rules	- message that user will see after using #rules command in chat

dcd.penalties	- contains punished hosts list and is updated every time op or sOP 
		punishes a person from a chat command, but it is read, 
		only when daemon starts. Every punishment must end with "\004\n"

		Syntaxt:
				IP:punished_nick:punisher_nick:penalties:start_time:duration:reason

				punished_nick - nick of person who was punished
				
				punisher_nick - nick of who punished person
				
				penalties:
						banned				+1
						no downloads		+2
						no public chat		+4
						no private chat		+8
						no search			+16
				
				start_time - the start time of penalty in "time(NULL);"
				
				duration: time in seconds

dcd.welcome	- message sent before login. Usefull to put rules.

dcd.hublinks	- hubs to link with. Syntax:
			IP:port:pass_in:pass_out

dcd.usercommands - $UserCommand lines shown to users after registration.

dcd.webchat	- the web chat page served by the built-in HTTP endpoint
			(default endpoint /, also /chat). One self-contained
			HTML file with CSS and JS inlined; edit it to
			customize - dcd re-reads it when it changes, and
			serves its built-in default copy if the file is
			missing.

dcd.webconsole	- the web console page (served at /console). Same rules as
			dcd.webchat.

nicks.allow	- patterns that nicks must match. Every line is one rule.

By default daemon binds to port 411(TCP/UDP) and telnet console to 511(TCP). 
The web endpoint (web console at /console, web chat at /, /healthcheck) is
disabled by default; set listen_port_http (e.g. 8080) in dcd.conf to enable it.
The web console bridges to the console port, so the browser behaves exactly
like the telnet console; the web chat logs in with accounts from dcd.users.

You can login to console:
$ telnet localhost 511

type help to get command list.

If you like you can use a main chat window in your DC client after connecting to the hub
type #help to get command list.

You can reconfigure @PACKAGE@ sending HUP signal to the process or rehash
command from telnet console.

